winewayland: let a captioned owned window be a subsurface AND still be draggable

The extension-panel docking bug (build tester, RC1 week day 1) is the Button
Editor root cause again, one condition away: a captioned owned window keeps a
valid owner_hint, is called managed because it has a caption, and becomes its
own xdg_toplevel. Wayland never tells a toplevel where it is, so a dock drag
crosses two independently placed surfaces and the drop lands somewhere the app
is not looking. Their NEUTRON_POPUP_TRACE capture shows it exactly:

    hwnd=0x50250 st=86cc0000 managed=1
    owner_hint=0x300f2 gwowner=0x300f2 owner_surf=(nil) toplevel=0x50250

The caption exclusion added in ...zzn was correct for the reason it gave -- a
subsurface is positioned by US, so the COMPOSITOR cannot move it, and anything
dragged by a title bar must stay a toplevel. But that reason is a consequence of
THIS driver's own SC_MOVE handling, not a property of Wayland.

WAYLAND_SysCommand sets ret = 0 for any window with wayland_win_data, while only
actually issuing xdg_toplevel_move when wayland_surface_is_toplevel(). Returning
0 means "handled" (win32u's defwnd.c: `if (!user_driver->pSysCommand(...)) return 0;`),
so for a subsurface the driver swallows SC_MOVE and does nothing at all: the
window becomes completely undraggable. That is what the caption rule was really
protecting against.

Decline it instead. When the surface is not a toplevel we return -1, win32u runs
its own sys_command_size_move() loop, and the resulting SetWindowPos flows through
the driver as a normal position update -- which for a subsurface is
wl_subsurface_set_position, i.e. exactly the coordinates we already control. The
window moves, and it moves under OUR control rather than the compositor's, which
is the Windows model this whole line of work has been restoring.

With that in place the caption exclusion has nothing left to protect, so owned
captioned windows adopt their owner like caption-less ones. Photoshop's Image
Size / Preferences / Export Settings keep their drag; the extension panel gains
a coordinate space its host can hit.

Gated: NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE_CAPTIONED=0 restores the ...zzn behaviour without
a rebuild, so this is A/B-able on a tester's machine like every other gate.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
@@ -310,6 +310,17 @@
     return on;
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON: include CAPTIONED owned windows in the owned-subsurface rule. Default ON since
+ * 2026-08-27, once WAYLAND_SysCommand stopped swallowing SC_MOVE for non-toplevels -- without
+ * that, a captioned subsurface could not be dragged at all, which is what the caption-less
+ * restriction was really protecting against. Set to 0 for caption-less-only. */
+static BOOL neutron_owned_subsurface_captioned(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) { const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE_CAPTIONED"); on = (e && e[0] == '0') ? 0 : 1; }
+    return on;
+}
+
 static BOOL wayland_win_data_create_wayland_surface(struct wayland_win_data *data,
                                                     struct wayland_surface *toplevel_surface,
                                                     struct wayland_surface *owner_surface,
@@ -786,15 +797,22 @@
              * value win32u actually passed us and it is correct. */
             if (!gwowner) gwowner = owner_hint;
 
-            /* ⛔ CAPTION-LESS ONLY. A subsurface is positioned by us, which is the whole point --
-             * but it also means the COMPOSITOR cannot move it, so anything the user drags by a
-             * title bar must stay an xdg_toplevel. A caption is exactly that signal. The user, on
-             * the panel this was written for: "This panel can't move and was never designed to
-             * move" -- so it loses nothing, while Image Size / Preferences / Export Settings keep
-             * the behaviour people expect. Captioned owned windows remain the business of
-             * NEUTRON_DOCK_OWNED above, which is opt-in for that reason. */
-            if (gwowner && gwowner != hwnd && !(st & WS_CAPTION) &&
-                NtUserIsWindowVisible( gwowner ))
+            /* CAPTIONED OWNED WINDOWS ARE INCLUDED as of 2026-08-27. The previous rule excluded
+             * them because a subsurface cannot be moved by the COMPOSITOR. True -- but the reason
+             * that mattered was WAYLAND_SysCommand claiming SC_MOVE for every window with wayland
+             * data and then doing nothing for a non-toplevel, which left such a window undraggable.
+             * That is fixed below: we decline, win32u runs its own move loop, and the SetWindowPos
+             * it produces reaches us as wl_subsurface_set_position. So a captioned owned window can
+             * be a subsurface AND still be dragged.
+             *
+             * What it buys: an owned window gets a coordinate space its owner can actually hit,
+             * which is what a dock drag needs. Premiere's extension panel could not be docked at
+             * all while it was an independent toplevel (build tester, 2026-08-27) -- the Button
+             * Editor root cause again, one condition away.
+             *
+             * Kill switch: NEUTRON_OWNED_SUBSURFACE_CAPTIONED=0 restores caption-less-only. */
+            if (gwowner && gwowner != hwnd && NtUserIsWindowVisible( gwowner ) &&
+                (!(st & WS_CAPTION) || neutron_owned_subsurface_captioned()))
             {
                 ERR( "neutron-ownedsub: hwnd=%p (style=%08x ex=%08x) -> SUBSURFACE of owner=%p "
                      "so its position is ours to set, not the compositor's\n",
@@ -1547,10 +1565,19 @@
                     xdg_toplevel_resize(surface->xdg_toplevel, wl_seat, button_serial,
                                         hittest_to_resize_edge(wparam & 0x0f));
                 }
+                /* NEUTRON: claim it only when the COMPOSITOR now owns the interaction. */
+                ret = 0;
             }
             pthread_mutex_unlock(&process_wayland.seat.mutex);
             wayland_win_data_release(data);
-            ret = 0;
+            /* NEUTRON: ret stays -1 when we did NOT hand the move/resize to the compositor.
+             * This used to claim EVERY window with wayland_win_data, including surfaces that are
+             * not toplevels -- and win32u treats 0 as "handled" (defwnd.c does
+             * "if (!user_driver->pSysCommand(...)) return 0;"), so a SUBSURFACE swallowed SC_MOVE
+             * and could not be dragged at all. Declining lets win32u run sys_command_size_move(),
+             * whose SetWindowPos reaches us as an ordinary position update; for a subsurface that
+             * is wl_subsurface_set_position -- the coordinates we already own. This is what makes
+             * a captioned owned window safe to turn into a subsurface above. */
         }
     }
 
